{"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/screenstates\/accessibility"]}],"metadata":{"modules":[{"name":"ScreenStates"}],"roleHeading":"Article","role":"article","title":"Accessibility"},"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Accessibility"},"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Everything the default placeholders — and your own custom ones — get for free: VoiceOver, Reduce Motion, Increase Contrast, Dynamic Type, and stable identifiers for XCUITest."}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates"]]},"kind":"article","seeAlsoSections":[{"anchor":"Related-Documentation","identifiers":["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenState","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithUIKit"],"title":"Related Documentation"}],"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"text":"Overview","anchor":"Overview","type":"heading","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"A placeholder is often the ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"only"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" thing on screen for a few hundred milliseconds, or the only thing a user with a slow connection sees for much longer than that — so it’s worth it being as accessible as the real content it stands in for. The default ","type":"text"},{"code":"ScreenStateDefault{Empty,Loading,Error}View","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"UIView","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"s handle all of this out of the box; this article rounds up what they do and how a fully custom placeholder can match them.","type":"text"}]},{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"VoiceOver announcements","anchor":"VoiceOver-announcements"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Each default placeholder announces its own appearance — "},{"code":"AccessibilityNotification.Announcement(_:).post()","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" in SwiftUI, "},{"code":"UIAccessibility.post(notification:.announcement, argument:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" in UIKit — so a screen settling into "},{"code":".empty","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"code":".loading","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", or "},{"code":".error","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is heard, not just seen.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenState\/accessibilityAnnouncement","type":"reference"},{"text":" exposes that same wording for any case, so a fully custom placeholder can announce transitions consistently instead of inventing its own text:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","code":[".onAppear {","    if let announcement = state.accessibilityAnnouncement {","        AccessibilityNotification.Announcement(announcement).post()","    }","}"],"type":"codeListing"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"It’s "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"nil"},{"type":"text","text":" for "},{"code":".data","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" — your own content is what VoiceOver should read at that point — and, for "},{"code":".empty","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", it’s the default “Nothing Here” text; announce your own custom title directly instead if your placeholder shows one, so VoiceOver matches what’s on screen. See "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithUIKit","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":" for the full examples."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Reduce Motion","anchor":"Reduce-Motion"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The default Loading placeholder’s ring spins via a repeating rotation animation — purely decorative, and exactly the kind of large, continuous motion Reduce Motion exists to suppress. "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStateDefaultLoadingView"},{"type":"text","text":" reads SwiftUI’s "},{"code":"accessibilityReduceMotion","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" environment value; "},{"code":"ScreenStateDefaultLoadingUIView","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" reads "},{"code":"UIAccessibility.isReduceMotionEnabled","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" and also observes "},{"code":"UIAccessibility.reduceMotionStatusDidChangeNotification","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", so an already-visible spinner responds immediately if the setting changes while on screen, not just on the next appearance. Either way, the ring stays static — still colorful, just not spinning — instead of disabling itself entirely, since a static ring still communicates “loading” on its own."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"heading","anchor":"Increase-Contrast","level":2,"text":"Increase Contrast"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"All three default placeholders’ gradient icons\/ring respect Increase Contrast — SwiftUI’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"colorSchemeContrast","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" environment value, UIKit’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"UIAccessibility.isDarkerSystemColorsEnabled","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" (observed live the same way Reduce Motion is). A multi-stop gradient can dip below an acceptable contrast ratio in places a single flat color never does, so when Increase Contrast is on, every gradient is swapped for a solid ","type":"text"},{"code":".label","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":".primary","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" tint instead.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"heading","anchor":"Dynamic-Type","level":2,"text":"Dynamic Type"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The default Empty\/Error placeholders’ text scales with Dynamic Type: SwiftUI’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"ScreenStateDefaultEmptyView","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"ErrorView","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" get this for free from ","type":"text"},{"code":"ContentUnavailableView","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"’s own text styles, and ","type":"text"},{"code":"ScreenStateDefaultEmptyUIView","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"code":"ErrorUIView","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"’s labels use "},{"code":"UIFont.preferredFont(forTextStyle: .body, compatibleWith:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":". Both are covered up to the largest accessibility size by snapshot tests, so a regression that silently pins the text to a fixed size would be caught. The default Loading placeholder has no text at all — its ring is a fixed-size shape, unaffected by Dynamic Type by design."}]},{"type":"heading","anchor":"Identifiers-for-XCUITest","level":2,"text":"Identifiers for XCUITest"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Every default placeholder exposes a stable "},{"code":"accessibilityIdentifier","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", independent of any localized or custom text, so a UI test can assert which state is showing without string-matching:"}]},{"rows":[[[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Identifier","type":"text"}]}],[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Shown on","type":"text"}]}]],[[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"screenStates.loading"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultLoadingView"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultLoadingUIView"}],"type":"paragraph"}]],[[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"screenStates.empty"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultEmptyView"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultEmptyUIView"}],"type":"paragraph"}]],[[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"screenStates.error"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultErrorView"},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultErrorUIView"}],"type":"paragraph"}]],[[{"inlineContent":[{"code":"screenStates.error.retryButton","type":"codeVoice"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The Retry button, when "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"onRetry"},{"type":"text","text":" is given"}],"type":"paragraph"}]],[[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"codeVoice","code":"screenStates.error.secondaryActionButton"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The secondary action button, when "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"secondaryAction"},{"type":"text","text":" is given"}],"type":"paragraph"}]]],"header":"row","type":"table"}]}],"sections":[],"references":{"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/ScreenState":{"url":"\/documentation\/screenstates\/screenstate","kind":"symbol","type":"topic","title":"ScreenState","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"enum"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"ScreenState"}],"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenS